  "prompt_text": "The figure organizes caloric materials by two dimensions: whether the material is single-phase or composite, and whether it is driven by one external field or by multiple fields. A small schematic matrix at the top defines four quadrants, then Venn-style diagrams show how magnetocaloric, mechanocaloric, and electrocaloric responses overlap for single-phase materials and for composites. The shaded overlap regions emphasize cases where two or three stimuli act together.\n\nThe larger lower part illustrates the underlying idea with simple block cartoons. In single-phase materials, one block responds directly to magnetic field, mechanical stress, or electric field, and can also be driven by combinations of these fields. In composite materials, the block is split into a caloric component and a transduction component, suggesting that one applied field can be converted into another internally, enabling indirect magnetic-, stress-, or electric-field-induced effects.\n\nAcross the figure, the main message is that caloric responses can be achieved either directly in one material or through coupled components in a composite, and that using multiple fields expands the possible ways to trigger entropy or temperature changes.",
